This repository contains two kinds of material under two different licences.
A repository with mixed material and a single LICENSE file is ambiguous
exactly when it matters, so both are stated explicitly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTENT — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Applies to everything under `content/`: the knowledge base entries and their
images and diagrams.

You may share and adapt this material, including commercially, provided you
give appropriate credit and license your derivatives under the same terms.

Full text: LICENSE-CONTENT.txt
Summary:   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CODE — MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Applies to everything else: `schema/`, `scripts/`, the workflow definitions
and the project configuration.

Full text: LICENSE-CODE.txt

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTRIBUTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Inbound equals outbound. By opening a pull request you offer your
contribution under the licence that applies to the files you changed —
CC BY-SA 4.0 for content, MIT for code. There is no separate contributor
licence agreement to sign.

This is stated rather than left implicit because "no licence yet" stops
being a neutral position the moment someone else's work is merged:
without it there is no right to redistribute their contribution, and
adding a licence afterwards needs every contributor's agreement.
